# Design: product positioning

## Problem
The README opened with mechanism ("keeps a local development
process running"). The phrase carried a laptop picture, did not say
who the tool is for, and did not say what it refuses.

## Approach
Claim the loop, not the orchestrator category. The practices report
(2026-10-11) documents the orchestrator category churning out and
dying when harnesses absorb it. The README names the machines
(laptop, server, headless VM) because unattended loops belong on
servers; the dev machine runs Looper controllers exactly that way.
PRODUCT.md now records target, wedge, and refusals.

Named-tool comparison sections ("Looper vs X") were deliberately
rejected: capability claims age, comparative claims rot, and the
dated research report already owns the comparison.

## Key Trade-offs
- "No opinions" is stated as a split: none about code, models, or
  process; strong ones about the loop. The unqualified claim is
  false (JSON task schema, publish gate, review cadence).
- Greenfield is named as best fit, not only fit; mature repos keep
  pull requests as their own choice.
